---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Software · Dario Picozzi"
  description="Open-source research software by Dario Picozzi: QuantumSymmetry and the interactive companion to the information geometry of large language models."
>
  <article class="measure">
    <header class="page-head">
      <h1>Software</h1>
      <p class="lede">Open-source code and interactive companions to the research.</p>
    </header>

    <section class="project">
      <img
        class="logo"
        src="/quantumsymmetry-logo.png"
        alt="QuantumSymmetry"
        width="300"
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <h2 class="sr-only">QuantumSymmetry</h2>
      <p class="install"><code>pip install quantumsymmetry</code></p>
      <p>
        A Python package for symmetry-adapted quantum simulation of molecules. Give it a
        molecular geometry and a basis set, and it automatically detects the molecule's
        Boolean symmetry group and constructs symmetry-adapted qubit operators, reducing
        the number of qubits needed to simulate the molecule. It integrates
        with <a href="https://pyscf.org">PySCF</a>, <a href="https://quantumai.google/openfermion">OpenFermion</a> and <a href="https://www.ibm.com/quantum/qiskit">Qiskit</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Since v0.3.0 it also includes a hardware-efficient
        universal ansatz with linear two-qubit-gate scaling whose quantum geometric metric
        is exactly diagonal in closed form, supporting natural-gradient VQE, real- and
        imaginary-time evolution and Haar sampling.
      </p>
      <p class="links">
        <a href="https://github.com/dariopicozzi/quantumsymmetry">GitHub</a>
        <a href="https://pypi.org/project/quantumsymmetry/">PyPI</a>
      </p>
      <details class="cite">
        <summary>How to cite</summary>
        <p>
          D. Picozzi and J. Tennyson,
          <em>Symmetry-adapted encodings for qubit number reduction by point-group and
          other Boolean symmetries</em>, Quantum Sci. Technol. <strong>8</strong> 035026
          (2023). <a href="https://doi.org/10.1088/2058-9565/acd86c">doi:10.1088/2058-9565/acd86c</a>
        </p>
      </details>
    </section>

    <section class="project" aria-labelledby="llm-geometry">
      <h2 class="project-title" id="llm-geometry">Information geometry of LLMs</h2>
      <p>
        Research code and an interactive companion for studying the geometry of
        language-model behaviour. The accompanying paper shows that the Fisher–Rao
        geometry of next-token probabilities captures structure shared across model
        architectures and shaped by training data. This geometry guides targeted
        changes to model behaviour while limiting disturbance to other predictions,
        with applications to steering, editing, attribution, dictionary learning and
        fine-tuning.
      </p>
      <p>
        Explore the findings through guided explanations and interactive figures,
        from the structure different models share to how it is learned and used to
        control their behaviour. Each study page links to downloadable experiment
        packages with code, inputs and instructions for reproducing the experiments.
      </p>
      <p class="links">
        <a href="https://llm-geometry.dariopicozzi.com/">Interactive companion</a>
        <a href="https://github.com/dariopicozzi/llm-geometry">GitHub</a>
        <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.11063">Paper</a>
      </p>
      <details class="cite">
        <summary>How to cite</summary>
        <p>
          D. Picozzi,
          <em>The information geometry of large language models is shared, learned,
          and controllable</em> (2026).
          <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.11063">arXiv:2609.11063</a>
        </p>
      </details>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

